First define popularity, because the whole system depends on it. It could be a raw count of events, a weighted score where a share counts more than a view, or a velocity measuring how fast engagement is rising. I pick a weighted score so a surge of lightweight views does not outrank genuinely engaging posts.

The single-process baseline is a hash map from item to count plus a min-heap of size K. Each event updates the map, and if the item's count beats the heap's smallest, the heap is updated. This is order N log K and is the mental model everything else scales out from.

```java
// Single-process top-K: HashMap counts + size-K min-heap
public List<String> topK(Stream<String> events, int k) {
    Map<String,Long> counts = new HashMap<>();
    events.forEach(e -> counts.merge(e, 1L, Long::sum));
    PriorityQueue<Map.Entry<String,Long>> heap =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Map.Entry.comparingByValue());
    for (var e : counts.entrySet()) {
        heap.offer(e);
        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();   // drop smallest
    }
    return heap.stream().sorted(Map.Entry.<String,Long>comparingByValue().reversed())
        .map(Map.Entry::getKey).toList();
}
```

To scale, events flow through Kafka partitioned by the item key, so every event for a given post lands on the same partition and therefore the same consumer. This is the crucial correctness property: each item has exactly one authoritative counting owner. The reason local-top-K-then-global-merge is exact only under this condition is a counterexample: if a post's counts were split across two workers, each worker might see that post below its local top-K and drop it, so the globally popular post appears in no local list and is missed entirely. With one owner per item, each worker's count for an item is the complete count, so merging local top-K lists cannot miss the true winner.

Rolling-window expiration uses time buckets or windowed stream processing. A tumbling window is non-overlapping fixed intervals, a hopping window overlaps, and a sliding window moves continuously; a five-minute rolling window refreshed every few seconds is naturally a hopping window. Late events and event-time versus processing-time are handled with watermarks: the system tracks event time, allows a bounded lateness so a slightly late event still lands in its correct window, and only finalizes a window once the watermark passes it. Duplicate events are handled with a stable event id and deduplication in checkpointed state so a replay does not double-count.

```java
// Deduplicate by stable eventId before counting
if (!seenEventIds.add(event.eventId())) return; // already counted, skip
window.add(event.itemId(), event.weight());
```

Hot keys, like a viral post, concentrate load on one partition, which is handled by partial pre-aggregation, key splitting into sub-keys that are summed later, or a dedicated hot-key path. Approximate counting with a Count-Min Sketch estimates frequencies in tiny memory but cannot by itself enumerate which item ids are the top-K, so it needs a candidate-generation stage; Space-Saving tracks heavy hitters directly. Snapshot publication writes a complete versioned snapshot and then atomically flips a pointer, and an older job must never overwrite a newer snapshot, enforced by comparing versions. The serving layer, typically Redis or a cache, exposes the precomputed snapshot along with freshness metadata like the generation time and watermark so a stale ranking is detectable. Eligibility filtering removes deleted, private, or policy-violating items, tie-breaking is deterministic by score descending then id ascending, and recovery relies on checkpointed stream state, event replay from Kafka, and idempotent sinks.

